{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793533163,"nonce":"OKnL73Ut/xnRe3C/JzkhQU5seVE=","title":"Owner decision 2026-11-01 (ADR-0077 grill, open question 7 / rank 4): build Raft-style term fencing for the scheduler lease.","message":"Today (VERIFIED): `leases` is (resource, owner, expires_at) only (migrations/0001_initial.sql:81). `lease()` (scarab-db-postgres lib.rs:2737) renews or takes over an expired lease. `is_leader` (scarab-engine scheduler.rs:2457) re-leases and then the tick acts; the leader-only writes (admit, sweep_unfinished_creations, reconcile_services, and the others gated on is_leader) do not re-check the lease inside their own transactions. A leader paused (SIGSTOP, GC, VM suspend) past the 30 s TTL resumes and acts once more before its next check, possibly after a peer took over.\n\nDesign:\n- `leases.term BIGINT`, incremented on every takeover (not on a renewal). `lease()` returns it.\n- Every leader-only write begins its transaction with `SELECT 1 FROM leases WHERE resource = 'scheduler' AND owner = $me AND term = $t FOR SHARE`; a miss is `DbError::NotLeader` and ends the tick. FOR SHARE makes a takeover's UPDATE wait for the transaction, so a stale leader's write is refused, never interleaved — no clock assumption.\n- Leader-only Db methods take a `LeaderTerm` value so a call without one does not compile.\n- Expand-contract migration: add the column with a default; old binaries ignore it (mixed-version window, see ce9d521's lesson).\nTests: functional tier on real Postgres — two schedulers on one database, A leads, A's lease expires, B takes over, A's leader-only write with its old term is refused and changes nothing. Live proof: ADR-0077 rank 4 (SIGSTOP the leader from the kind node past the TTL, SIGCONT; checker I1 + I5 clean).\nFirst step: list every is_leader-gated path and every Db write it reaches; if it is large, split by path.","files":null}]}